ield-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.6$ to $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.6,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.6) & =6(0.6)^{5}-(0.6)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.077\\,76-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.466\\,56-0.046\\,656 \\\\  & =0.419\\,904 \\\\  & ≈0.420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.4) & =6(0.4)^{5}-(0.4)^{6} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0.010\\,24-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.061\\,44-0.004\\,096 \\\\  & =0.057\\,344 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.6$ to $0.4.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.6}{0.4}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={1.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.419\\,904$ to $0.057\\,344.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.419\\,904}{0.057\\,344}=7.322\\,54…≈7.323.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1.5}^{p+1} & ≈7.322\\,54… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{1.5}⁡(7.322\\,54…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.910… \\\\ p & ≈3.910….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{5}-(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334710/ma-source-field/5f8579eb6bc698969b5742be8d4c4aabd68c07de1e713258dea3f972241a8513" "q-334710/ma-source-field/c72a46c89b0c12e2bfb817d309489e683f6843de54f6a349afd2a9db46c61d48" "q-334710/ma-source-field/984102576587e8f869b2c9064faa073e4e2d31a340657d4db91c1486d32eb88b" "q-334710/ma-source-field/f8c0a144678d0871af0d9d69afff694a6a471ef723967e0e0fc5f6434bd6f615"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334710"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/ma-source-field/60a54c9433397c35fea0913f85719fd7ce1450af71a73302f571ba04a2e7a73b/02e0a36f18c94973e1e96e511c218ec290df6ccd6e36a09919563b5a9dbce536" :answer/id #uuid "af30b4d5-ae13-5e76-aef4-2c8e10d268d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.016" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/ma-source-field/60a54c9433397c35fea0913f85719fd7ce1450af71a73302f571ba04a2e7a73b/be2996522c88880b85c94a2df96123acadb7ac9f8da34e41793102bb8efe9a83" :answer/id #uuid "5a6cdaa2-9b9d-5112-9911-3369aa53afb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.075" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/ma-source-field/60a54c9433397c35fea0913f85719fd7ce1450af71a73302f571ba04a2e7a73b/9fada88b7612c02337ec829dd89f2c6a29c9c4398a8681f279b5635642815f62" :answer/id #uuid "0d4d56fd-96cd-5769-9c15-8ba42c5adc88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.281" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334724/ma-source-field/60a54c9433397c35fea0913f85719fd7ce1450af71a73302f571ba04a2e7a73b/b34544a9837d181994c7a132937df1564b327d325fab23b2c613d8bcfe8cb599" :answer/id #uuid "14a16a56-934d-5ec6-8e