alidate]} {:db/id "q-334724" :question/id #uuid "2c78fbb9-dc1c-5d53-be56-9ae880822440" :question/math-academy-id "q-334724" :question/problem "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =8(0.5)^{5}+2(0.5)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.031\\,25+2\\cdot 0.015\\,625 \\\\  & =0.281\\,25 \\\\  & ≈0.281.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.2) & =8(0.2)^{5}+2(0.2)^{6} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 0.000\\,32+2\\cdot 0.000\\,064 \\\\  & =0.002\\,688 \\\\  & ≈0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.2}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2.5}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.281\\,25$ to $0.002\\,688.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.281\\,25}{0.002\\,688}=104.631\\,69…≈104.632.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2.5}^{p+1} & ≈104.631\\,69… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(104.631\\,69…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈5.075… \\\\ p & ≈4.075….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=8(Δx)^{5}+2(Δx)^{6}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{5})=O\\,((Δx)^{4+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334724/ma-source-field/60a54c9433397c35fea0913f85719fd7ce1450af71a73302f571ba04a2e7a73b" "q-334724/ma-source-field/3868ac914f80224cdc7abfb682316f80683cb9b17d3c901cb4723c2e97678b38" "q-334724/ma-source-field/9ac050fe4b2e49fbbd3e13180e1f176e8a3b74593bea2c4ea915a559ecaa629f" "q-334724/ma-source-field/f8c0a144678d0871af0d9d69afff694a6a471ef723967e0e0fc5f6434bd6f615"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42c41150-5104-58cc-927b-f55b94529cb2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334724"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186167254 :question/answer-fields 17592186164604] {:db/id "q-334754/ma-source-field/1954c5a9e180875845c7923ed4d8e82ab452802753fd1e493997117a469351b9/ca5d67c850982ef213b55d1f05c1982c01758138b8779ef65a4059c3a38ed0c8" :answer/id #uuid "b05325f4-1482-56b6-8efe-ae71cef71608" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "approaches" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334754/ma-source-field/1954c5a9e180875845c7923ed4d8e82ab452802753fd1e493997117a469351b9/a4f36f295ffdcc94188a6fa54e6a58e804da1e24f5661638790f18badaa10aa2" :answer/id #uuid "c6ca0294-aea3-59be-887c-efcec2803859" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "circles around" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334754/ma-source-field/1954c5a9e180875845c7923ed4d8e82ab452802753fd1e493997117a469351b9" :an