51-6c19-5b71-8fd7-eed9401b1f21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the chain rule" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7/0d7dc7ec655a0c82bd7741f9fba57b723125dbb773b97254c25e0e630ce3db2b" :answer/id #uuid "0fc76cf1-03a1-556f-8931-c7d7ae719952" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the linearity of the Laplace transform" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7/90d0135f4c41887ba2a3b5b493e3b493c377d20daa69e5bdaabe55b63d4f90b0" :answer/id #uuid "97a57549-7948-51f0-8af3-6bc0134199b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the product rule" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7" :answer-field/id #uuid "a33fd5fc-c3b7-5ab6-a7f4-eb3d87c63a29" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7/189a1bc3918431f8acd240b0382354cbb34f876b1bb143121ec10168178d54ea" "q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7/0d7dc7ec655a0c82bd7741f9fba57b723125dbb773b97254c25e0e630ce3db2b" "q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7/90d0135f4c41887ba2a3b5b493e3b493c377d20daa69e5bdaabe55b63d4f90b0"] :answer-field/correct "q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7/189a1bc3918431f8acd240b0382354cbb34f876b1bb143121ec10168178d54ea" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "40ff4752-2fb6-5924-9814-2ff991abb48a"] :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))=f(t)\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,\\frac{∂}{∂s}(-t{e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,({t}^{2}{e}^{-st})={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{the chain rule}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334874/ma-source-field/74e1e11f6b59c6d2bce7a3f36592db58cfe1aad1c727cc449679030d685a901e" "q-334874/ma-source-field/34ce9c2008a222a188876a4986f833751aa36952ceaea19289280fe51d6526aa" "q-334874/ma-source-field/0c56e046246b204b030cac02ee3f200f31629ed73a2564b7a78342a3d7f6e9f7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334879/ma-source-field/74e1e11f6b59c6d2bce7a3f36592db58cfe1aad1c727cc449679030d685a901e/53d82fe9de28f0ed63fb1ded8f44402dee96fce0effb12b68e34d31a340d63a7" :answer/id #uuid "0cbb1d3c-3189-59cd-9bda-454fb8b2eebc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334879/ma-source-field/74e1e11f6b59c6d2bce7a3f36592db58cfe1aad1c727cc449679030d685a901e/9c65e771a257723f199dd36fcb93f8c4cd41ba850ff9c3d2716b7642d6951fbb" :answer/id #uuid "050780e1-44f8-5c5a-b0da-175e8229eda4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334879/ma-source-field/74e1e11f6b59c6d2bce7a3f36592db58cfe1aad1c727cc449679030d685a901e/30e11553a14c4b724190c38b898a039988015e55be021b10f0364e3d70ce3b98" :answer/id #uuid "9cb0ad98-5f4e-54e0-9915-621ebc30cb8f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:d