:answer.type/text :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334879/ma-source-field/e7357a779cdf5f302401186c63eab9b5d0f288d3bd0a203d7618bc2ea89df641/cd523c603059a95ce1d883bdf67190528410757e58e4c50d15fa4e8f97d77665" :answer/id #uuid "85dade51-274f-5498-850c-ab5af3ef7514" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "substitution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334879/ma-source-field/e7357a779cdf5f302401186c63eab9b5d0f288d3bd0a203d7618bc2ea89df641" :answer-field/id #uuid "75b69391-8c29-5787-b8c3-1abe4a208951" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-334879/ma-source-field/e7357a779cdf5f302401186c63eab9b5d0f288d3bd0a203d7618bc2ea89df641/8f4a587b01f8cb8d063fadf22cff3d81718f4f308670bfb36807fee887123832" "q-334879/ma-source-field/e7357a779cdf5f302401186c63eab9b5d0f288d3bd0a203d7618bc2ea89df641/9c65e771a257723f199dd36fcb93f8c4cd41ba850ff9c3d2716b7642d6951fbb" "q-334879/ma-source-field/e7357a779cdf5f302401186c63eab9b5d0f288d3bd0a203d7618bc2ea89df641/cd523c603059a95ce1d883bdf67190528410757e58e4c50d15fa4e8f97d77665"] :answer-field/correct "q-334879/ma-source-field/e7357a779cdf5f302401186c63eab9b5d0f288d3bd0a203d7618bc2ea89df641/cd523c603059a95ce1d883bdf67190528410757e58e4c50d15fa4e8f97d77665" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334879" :question/id #uuid "5b669529-7292-59be-8712-06b8b95e08ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-334879" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then $F(s)$ is twice differentiable for all $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$  \n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-334879/ma-source-field/74e1e11f6b59c6d2bce7a3f36592db58cfe1aad1c727cc449679030d685a901e" "q-334879/ma-source-field/9c181ca3b5d3d90e8a714fe10d84c10d591a11491985178b322c4f74158605d0" "q-334879/ma-source-field/e7357a779cdf5f302401186c63eab9b5d0f288d3bd0a203d7618bc2ea89df641"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334879"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334924/ma-source-field/339271da1f138101a62f343dbea4c6c036fbcaa87693a6b4f8456723c6546447/bd3b86eae8db69f9b7a577573cb57c05a23a9d978ac30e968f40636dad3def03" :answer/id #uuid "f37ded06-8f51-502f-9066-f2e3021a9d5c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+s))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334924/ma-source-field/339271da1f138101a62f343dbea4c6c036fbcaa87693a6b4f8456723c6546447/52335f0edfd34c58cdde7c5b19af5b6e882d935ae07c7d1b7cd1f9d0211b5b9a" :answer/id #uuid "6d012455-60c7-55cd-8abe-a5ae87c14843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-s))" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334924/ma-source-field/339271da1f138101a62f343dbea4c6c036fbcaa87693a6b4f8456723c6546447/c467d1cc7f4d2e58aa805c71fd1692e1e1677664421b39f15a9130bb55a71a0c" :answer/id #uuid "61927b3b-fbb4-54e5-bcfd-ea2b2a79e95e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answe